nodecode-kokoro-tts is a rewrite of brunosouzas's brunosouzas/hermes-kokoro-tts:

  https://github.com/brunosouzas/hermes-kokoro-tts
  commit d8e8769d6997f29f98da7ceea97b4e3a76688b39
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Bruno Souza (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the whole repository at that commit: a Hermes
text-to-speech provider that spoke through a Kokoro server of its own on
127.0.0.1, with the server, an installer and a CLI beside it. Its speech wire
is carried over unchanged: POST /v1/audio/speech with input, voice, speed and
response_format, the voice's own first letter as lang_code unless a language
overrides it, English loanwords handed to a non-English voice as english_words
and english_accent, and GET /v1/voices for what the model holds. So are the
voice id rule (a language letter, a gender letter and a name, so a voice can
never name a file), the per-sentence language plan -- English always detected,
Portuguese always, Italian only when it has a voice; a sentence with no clear
signal keeps the voice before it; neighbours that would use one voice are one
request -- the function-word and accent scoring it decides with, and the
joining of several requests into one WAV. LICENSE is the repository's LICENSE,
unchanged.

What was left out, and why: the installer and the service manager (a uv
environment on Python 3.11, the pinned model snapshot, a launchd plist or a
systemd user unit, the install/status/restart/uninstall verbs), because a cell
does not bundle Python or a virtual environment and calls the server the user
already runs -- the README says how; the server itself (server/kokoro_server.py,
its MLX and torch backends), for the same reason; its English-loanword
phonemizer, which runs inside the server, so the cell forwards the words
instead of doing the G2P; and the Hermes provider registration, because
Nodecode's provider seam is models, not voices -- the two verbs a user reaches
for are the cell's own tools, kokoro_speak and kokoro_voices, with /kokoro and
(help :kokoro) beside them.
